      keyPoints: [
        "Gustav Stresemann: Chancellor in 1923, Foreign Minister until his death in October 1929.",
        "Rentenmark (1923), Dawes Plan (1924), Locarno (1925), League of Nations (1926), Young Plan (1929).",
        "Culture flourished and extremists faded: Nazis won 12 seats (2.6%) in 1928.",
        "The recovery depended on short-term American loans — ‘dancing on a volcano’.",
      ],
      whyItWorks:
        "Stresemann restored confidence: a stable currency made Germany worth lending to, and peaceful diplomacy made France willing to compromise. That confidence is why the Nazis looked like a fringe party in 1928. But because prosperity rested on borrowed American money, Germany's fate was tied to the US economy — so a crash in New York could, and did, bring down German democracy.",
